      },
      "body": "Per the current spec:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3902892/122455113-e7574b80-cf60-11eb-8fe5-c36ae35137e3.png)\r\n\r\nThis passes along the `Yield` and `Await` parameters to _Initializer_, which seems incorrect. A class field is wrapped in an implicit function, per https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-runtime-semantics-classfielddefinitionevaluation, step 3.e:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3902892/122455317-1bcb0780-cf61-11eb-9932-a1f3d38054a5.png)\r\n\r\nSo a _YieldExpression_ and _AwaitExpression_ should not be valid in a class initializer.\r\n\r\nHere is an example to illustrate the issue:\r\n```js\r\nasync function f(p) {\r\n  return class C {\r\n    x = await p; // when could this possibly be awaited?\r\n  };\r\n}\r\nlet C = await f(Promise.resolve(1));\r\nlet obj = new C();\r\nobj.x; // what is this value?\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt seems to me that `Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]` should be `Initializer[+In, ~Yield, ~Await]`. ",
      "closed_by": null,
      "reactions": {
